{"language": "en", "language_probability": 0.9975, "duration": 89.815, "text": "Most people think productivity is about doing more, that is the biggest lie of the last decade. I spent three years tracking every minute of my day, and the data destroyed everything I believed. Here is the shocking part, the top 1 percent do not work more hours. They work in blocks of 90 minutes, then they stop completely. When I tried this for 30 days, my output doubled while my working hours dropped by 40 percent. The reason is simple, your brain has a 90-minute ultradion rhythm, and after that your focus collapses whether you feel tired or not. So the rule is brutal, 90 minutes of deep work, 20 minutes of complete silence, no phone, no music, nothing. The second thing nobody tells you is that your morning routine is probably making you slower. I used to wake up and immediately check messages that single habit cost me almost two hours of focus every single day. Now I wait 60 minutes before touching my phone, and the difference is night and day, and the third thing, this one is going to hurt, multitasking is not a skill. It is a tax. Every time you switch tasks, you lose about 23 minutes of real focus, 23 minutes. So if you switch five times a day, you have lost two hours before lunch. Here is what I want you to do today. Pick one task, set a timer for 90 minutes, put your phone in another room, that is it, no apps, no system, no journal, just one block of undistracted work. Do that for a week and message me what happened because I promise you will not go back.", "segments": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 4.3, "text": "Most people think productivity is about doing more, that is the biggest lie of the", "words": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.16, "word": " Most", "probability": 0.5719}, {"start": 0.16, "end": 0.54, "word": " people", "probability": 0.9968}, {"start": 0.54, "end": 0.88, "word": " think", "probability": 0.9962}, {"start": 0.88, "end": 1.42, "word": " productivity", "probability": 0.991